mode: "100644"
text: "# The reality of before/after content in 2026 (verify-quarterly)\n\nThe FTC rules here are law, not trend — attribute them, and mark only the shifting enforcement details\nverify-quarterly. The craft principles (the middle is the content; consistent conditions; honesty is the edge)\nare stable.\n\n## Why the format is so powerful — and so distrusted\n- A before/after is **visual, built-in proof** and inherently satisfying (the reveal); it over-indexes on saves,\n  shares, and completion.\n- It's also the **single most abused and distrusted** format — staged befores, flattering-vs-unflattering\n  lighting/posing, unrealistic timelines, undisclosed edits/filters/aids. Audiences are trained to be skeptical,\n  which is exactly why **honesty is the competitive edge:** a believable transformation (real timeline, consistent\n  conditions, the real messy journey) beats a suspiciously-perfect fake that trips the skepticism reflex.\n\n## The FTC spine (this is law — attribute, verify-quarterly for enforcement)\nBefore/after photos + testimonials are, in the FTC's words, an enforcement \"favorite.\" The rules (16 CFR Part 255\n+ Health Products Compliance Guidance):\n- **A before/after implies the result is *typical*.** Using it, you must either show typical results **or** clearly\n  and conspicuously **disclose the actual typical/generally-expected results** — and have substantiation for them.\n- **\"Results not typical\" / \"results vary\" is NOT enough.** If the result isn't typical, you must disclose the\n  **actual** typical result. (FTC Gut Check.)\n- **Use the median, not an outlier-skewed mean**, for the typical figure; **no survivorship** — you can't base\n  \"typical\" only on those who stuck with it, ignoring quitters.\n- **\"Net impression\" test:** visuals **alone** can be a deceptive claim — a lighting/posing/edit trick is itself a\n  false claim even with no words.\n- **\"Actual consumers\":** if it implies real customers, use real customers **or** disclose they're not (paid\n  actors/stock). Fabricated/bogus endorsements are deceptive.\n- **Health claims** need **competent and reliable scientific evidence** (RCTs); weight loss > ~1 lb/week over a\n  long period is unusual, and \"lose weight without effort\" is deception.\n- **Clear and conspicuous** = it stands out, sits close to the claim, and alters the net impression — not buried\n  in hashtags/footnotes.\n- **Live enforcement:** the FTC's 2025 NextMed action penalized a firm for paid-actor testimonials, before/after\n  photos sourced off Craigslist, and an unsubstantiated \"average 53 lbs\" it didn't track. (attribute;\n  verify-quarterly.)\n\n## The craft edge (stable)\n**The middle is the content** — endpoints alone are a flex; the process, setbacks, and real timeline build\nrelatability, credibility, and value. **Isolate the variables** (same angle/lighting/framing) so the change is\nreal. **Tie it to the viewer** — a relatable before + an attainable, honestly-framed after + an actionable\ntakeaway.\n\n## House metrics\nSaves/shares + watch-through + profile visits/inquiries — native only; never fabricate a result, timeline, or\naverage.\n"
